From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oyvind Harboe <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
Date: Sun, 14 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] BUILD: do not link -latomic on Linux

The BCR overlay links -latomic unconditionally on Linux. That is a
libgcc-era assumption: libatomic is GCC's out-of-line fallback for
atomic operations the hardware cannot do inline, and glibc-based
distributions ship it next to libgcc.

The BCR "llvm" toolchain (hermetic-llvm) is zero-sysroot: its runtime is
compiler-rt and it ships no libatomic at all, so the link fails outright
with

    ld.lld: error: unable to find library -latomic

Nothing in verilator needs it there. Every atomic in the sources is on a
type clang lowers inline on x86-64 and aarch64, and the out-of-line
__atomic_* calls that would require the library are never emitted. On a
toolchain that does ship libatomic, dropping the flag is equally safe:
the library is a fallback, not a dependency, and an unused -l is exactly
what it costs.

Fixing it here rather than upstream because the flag is correct for the
toolchain the overlay was written against; the general fix is a select
on the runtime, which is a conversation for the BCR module rather than a
condition this study should invent. Retire this patch at a verilator BCR
revision that makes -latomic conditional.

Not upstreamed.
---
 BUILD.bazel | 2 --
 1 file changed, 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/BUILD.bazel b/BUILD.bazel
--- a/BUILD.bazel
+++ b/BUILD.bazel
@@ -473,7 +473,6 @@
     linkopts = select({
         "@platforms//os:linux": [
             "-lpthread",
-            "-latomic",
         ],
         "@platforms//os:macos": [
             "-lpthread",
@@ -537,7 +536,6 @@
     linkopts = select({
         "@platforms//os:linux": [
             "-lpthread",
-            "-latomic",
         ],
         "@platforms//os:macos": [
             "-lpthread",
